<!--
    kuna: a 64-bit return whose high half the function zeroes keeps its whole
    register on RISC-V (GH-865).  The procedure-call standard sign-extends
    every 32-bit return value to the register, unsigned ones too, so a
    function that zero-extends a word it may set the sign bit of returns a
    64-bit value.  Sub-variable flow used to trim the RETURN to the word, and
    the function printed as returning int: a caller widening the result
    sign-extended what the binary zero-extends.

    Stage / sub-stage: P3 sub-variable flow, the RETURN pull
    (p3_dataflow/subflow.rs try_return_pull, p5_types/kuna_zextreturn.rs
    zero_extended_word), under the rule p4_calls/kuna_narrowext.rs states.
    Two passes: option narrowext off is the compiler spec's zero extension
    (the bug), the default abi is the fix.

    Source (clang -target riscv64-linux-gnu -march=rv64gc -O2, bytes verbatim):

        unsigned long z32m(unsigned long x) { return (x * 3) & 0xffffffffUL; }
        unsigned long zu(unsigned int x) { return x; }
        unsigned long zc(unsigned long x, unsigned y) {
          if (x > 5) return y * 7;
          return 0xfffffff0UL;
        }
        unsigned ru(unsigned x) { return x * 3; }
        unsigned short rus(unsigned long x) { return x * 3; }

    z32m and zu end slli a0,a0,32; srli a0,a0,32, and zc returns either
    that or the constant 0xfffffff0.  ru ends addw, which sign-extends, and
    rus zero-extends a halfword, which the rule extends by its type's sign:
    both keep their narrow return either way.
-->
